To create an AWS IAM role that an EC2 instance can assume, you can use the `aws_iam_role` resource in Terraform. Below is an example of how you can define such a role:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"  # Replace with your desired region
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "example" {
  name = "example-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })

  tags = {
    Name = "ExampleRole"
  }
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "example" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.example.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole"
}
```

### Explanation:

1. **Provider Configuration**: The `provider "aws"` block is used to configure the AWS provider. Replace `"us-west-2"` with the desired AWS region.

2. **IAM Role Definition**: The `aws_iam_role` resource is defined with the name `example`. The `assume_role_policy` block specifies the policy that allows the EC2 instance to assume the role. In this case, it allows the role to be assumed by the `ec2.amazonaws.com` service.

